o i see folks replant big trees  um sometimes i'll see small trees but  you know i see very few big trees being  replanted in our communities and that's  something that you know we're going to  need to work on reversing that trend  and bioeconomy is also really important  so this  use of durable wood products  supporting our forest industry and the  creation of those can be essential if  we're going to you know continue having  good management on our landscape  um also you know these lumber products  are sequestering or you know have  sequestered carbon um while the you know  substitute products they have like  concrete or steel you know uh take quite  a bit of carbon to create and so you  know there's a real a lot to be gained  by using these products and there are  definitely a lot of hurdles in terms of  the construction industry  what they are used to using standard  practices and getting these practices  out in the landscape i think mass timber  it has a real potential to make a big  impact if we can  get more adoption from  developers when making properties when  making buildings  so the last section of my talk today i  just want to talk about some  opportunities that landowners have a lot  of these  programs have been running long-standing  programs but we have some new programs  at dec  that can help landowners  manage their properties  you know there's a lot of things that  we've recommended in the clcpa to help  improve  forest management on the landscape  and a lot of those haven't been  implemented yet so there's a lot to be  gained  you know a lot of things that hopefully  will be coming down the line um but this  is you know kind of what's available  right now and so i'm going to run  through a couple dec programs and touch  briefly on  the natural resource conservation  services program which is uh  the environmental quality incentives  program equip it's it's quite a long  name but um  you know that has is a long-standing  program um that  you know helps people do improved force  management on the property so  um equip has a forced stand improvement  practice  which helps people you know improve the  sustained forced health and productivity  and one of the newer  practices that they're allowing here is  increase or maintain carbon storage  in their forests is another  practice and so  you need to talk with a professional  forester if you want to get involved in  these programs or your local nrcs office  this is a grant program and i i  apologize i'm not sure when they they  open up their program but um  it's  it's also cost share so i think  landowners do have to  do some contributing to this kind of a  program  our dc's for stewardship program is  long-standing it connects to landowners  across the landscape with professional  forestry advice  and management advice dec land or forest  staff will go out and walk the land with  the landowner and work on  either putting together a paper  management plan for them or giving them  some technical assistance and basic  guidance  based on availability of our staff  unfortunately in reduction in the amount  of staff that we have to do this  you know we don't do a full inventory  and stuff that a consulting forester  would do and so after you know  connecting with the land owner and  finding out what their goals for  management are and what they're looking  to do we often refer them to a  cooperating forester private consultant  who works on a fee basis with them  for a lot of what they want to do if  you're looking to do a timber harvest we  always recommend working with a  consulting forester  to get the best outcome that you're  looking for on that harvest  our 4da program as i talked about a  little bit earlier needs to be  updated but it is still a really  beneficial program  it provides an 80 reduction in the  assessed that value of forest land that  someone owns that's committed to this  program it's a rolling 10-year  commitment and so every year that you  get the benefit you are  enrolled for another 10 years out from  that and has put the lien on the deed  and so it really is sort of like a con  temporary conservation easement on those  properties  but it also you know connects those  landowners with the tools that they need  to properly manage their properties  through professional forester force  and  and dec oversight in terms of reviewing  those management plans and prescriptions  for those harvests  but the big crux here is that landowners  have to have at least 50 acres to commit  and you know there are a lot of  requirements of the program so it's  really important that landowners know  what they're getting into when they sign  up for this kind of a program  but you know only nine percent of new  york forests are enrolled in this um  so it's  really a small percentage of new york  forests and so we need to really expand  this in order to  make a big impact out on the landscape  just a few other programs we run our  trees for trips programs buffer in a bag  through the saratoga tree nursery  provides  trees for land  for landowners who are looking to  reforest  reforest riparian areas our saratoga  tree nursery does sealing sales each  year and sells  pretty cheap ceilings to folks across  new york  and our urban forestry grant program is  available to municipalities and um and  non-profits  uh to either get urban management plans  do tree planting um  work and and things like that so really  is working in that space and i think  that the newest  round of that grant is going to be  released sometime in 2022.  and then i just want to talk briefly  about our newest program that where we  started we just finished our first round  in new york which is regenerate new york  which is really you know targeting some  of this climate work  and  um  we had a few out four eligible practices  here a4 station reforestation stand  restoration competing controlling  competing vegetation and deer enclosures  and this is a  um  the round one had about 450 000 dollars  that we allocated to landowners between  that at least three thousand to fifty  thousand dollars um  in money for that there was a 25 percent  match for landowners so they had to  match some of the work that was  happening out there but it you know  helps get some some of this work out in  the landscape and get it started you  know unfortunately when thinking about  the impact of this program that 450 000  got us about 500 acres of impacted sites  so we're going to  if we want to reforest a million acres  you know this might not be the easiest  way to do it is getting a lot of that  work done we're going to really need to  ramp up what we're doing  and then i  really briefly talked about invasive  species i mentioned we have a bureau of  invasive species and ecosystem health in  dec  and  we've got folks out there surveying for  invasive species we do really rely on  citizen scientists and i just wanted to  mention here imap invasives is a great  resource for folks if you don't know  about it it's a way to report invasive  species that you see  all your submissions will be reviewed so  if you're not sure what it is you can  still put it in and a dec staff member  is going to look at it and confirm or  deny  what you see out there and so that's a  great resource for folks to connect them  with some technical experts  our forest health email is also a way to  email pictures and questions and  there are local  partnerships for regional invasive  species management or prisms  that are grassroots networks for  managing invasive species across new  york that dec funds  and our local experts at some of the  invasive species in your area so if  you've got invasive species questions  there are a lot of resources out there  for you  and  dec is here to help as well as our  partners  that is all that i had today  that was fantastic brian thank you so  much  peter will field some questions and i  will set out a poll  yes thanks very much brian and and  thanks back to tim also for a lot of  great information  we have a question  uh  please discuss how the clcpa limits  offset possibilities  yeah so i mentioned this briefly and i'm  not an expert in the offset  field but in terms of  i know that the clcpa in terms of its  mandatory if you were to set up a  mandatory market where folks are able to  offset  um it limits the distance that those  offsets could be  placed from  um  from where that emission source is  and so  you know that's a  important part of it i think also it's  important to note that in terms of you  know statewide sequestration and carbon  neutrality goal by 2050  we're not looking at offsets it's more  the total sequestration  rate for new york forest  and so that's an important distinction  there  great thanks  another question  has the department of lands and forests  consulted with the department of ag and  markets on goals for aforestation  or reforestation of agricultural areas  are there a specific strategies  so this is a great um outcome from the  uh 4g panel as it was um  it was working directly with ag markets  on this and so you know we we've  collaborated quite a bit in terms of  that we haven't we're still working on  creating prioritization of areas um but  you know and i think some of the work  that's been done by  private groups on tnc for example you  know trying to identify those marginal  lands that are not currently in  agricultural production that would be um  ideal for creating forests and we have  definitely worked with  um worked with agon markets and and will  continue to especially with their soil  and water conservation districts with uh  which are um  really have a lot of on the ground  knowledge on in this area  great thanks so much  uh here's another question what is the  status of 480b and 480c  are they enacted where can we get more  information  um so those were recommended by the  forestry panel 480b and 4dc they are not  enacted they  um  i will say he